<TopicPage slug="subjects/finance/intermediate/financial-statements">
  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    A company is a process that never stops — invoices, payrolls, factories, loans — and a
    financial statement is a frozen frame of that process, prepared according to rules strict
    enough that two strangers can compare two companies without meeting either. There are
    three frames, and each answers a different question. The <strong>income
    statement</strong> answers "how did the business perform over this period?". The
    <strong>balance sheet</strong> answers "what does it own and owe right now?". The
    <strong>cash flow statement</strong> answers "where did money actually move?". Read
    separately, each one misleads in a characteristic way; read together, they reveal the
    business — its model, its health, and sometimes its crimes. This page builds each
    statement, shows the accounting choices that shape them, and then assembles the ratio
    toolkit professionals use to interrogate all three at once.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="the three statements and how they interlock" widget="equationStep" config={stmtsCfg} />

  <h2>The income statement: performance over a period</h2>
  <p>
    The income statement (profit-and-loss statement) covers an interval — a quarter, a year —
    and runs one line at a time down to the bottom. <strong>Revenue</strong> (the top line)
    is what customers were charged. Subtract the <strong>cost of goods sold</strong> (COGS) —
    materials, direct labour — and you reach <strong>gross profit</strong>; gross margin
    (gross profit ÷ revenue) is the purest available read on pricing power: a software
    company keeping 70–80 cents of every sales dollar and a grocery chain keeping 2–3 are
    playing entirely different games with entirely different defences. Subtract operating
    expenses (salaries, rent, marketing, R&amp;D, depreciation) to reach
    <strong>operating profit</strong> (EBIT) — the profit of the business itself, before the
    company's financing choices muddy the water. Subtract interest, add or subtract tax, and
    you arrive at <strong>net income</strong>, the bottom line. The staircase structure is the
    point: you can see <em>where</em> profit was made or lost, not just how much.
  </p>
  <p>
    A worked sketch: a retailer sells for 100, having bought the goods for 60 (gross profit
    40, margin 40 per cent); runs stores and staff for 25 (operating profit 15); pays 2 of
    interest and 4 of tax, netting 9. From these few numbers you already know more than the
    headline: that a 5-point squeeze in gross margin would more than halve profit — the
    operating leverage of thin-margin businesses.
  </p>

  <h2>The balance sheet: a photograph at an instant</h2>
  <p>
    The balance sheet freezes one moment and lists everything the company <em>owns</em>
    (assets), everything it <em>owes</em> (liabilities), and the residual that belongs to
    shareholders (equity), bound by the identity that never fails:
    <strong>Assets = Liabilities + Equity</strong>. Every transaction in the company's life
    touches this equation twice — that is <em>double-entry bookkeeping</em>, invented by
    Luca Pacioli in 1494 — which is what gives accounts their self-checking arithmetic.
    Assets and liabilities are each split by time horizon: <strong>current</strong> items are
    those expected to convert or fall due within a year (cash, inventory, receivables;
    payables, short-term debt), and the difference between current assets and current
    liabilities is <strong>working capital</strong> — the fuel tank that keeps daily
    operations running. Non-current assets include property, plant and equipment, and — the
    accountant's most honest confession — <strong>goodwill and intangibles</strong>: when a
    company buys another for more than the book value of its assets, the excess is parked on
    the balance sheet as goodwill, a number that is really a receipt for hope.
  </p>
  <p>
    <em>Common misconception:</em> the balance sheet's numbers are the company's value. They
    are mostly <em>historical cost</em> — what things were paid for, minus depreciation. A
    brand built over decades may appear at roughly zero; a factory's land may appear at its
    1962 price. Book equity and economic value are different animals, and the gap between
    them is precisely why markets exist.
  </p>

  <h2>The cash flow statement: the truth serum</h2>
  <p>
    The third statement abandons opinion entirely. Every dollar that entered or left is
    classified into three streams. <strong>Operating cash flow</strong> (OCF): cash from the
    actual business — customer receipts minus supplier, employee and tax payments.
    <strong>Investing cash flow</strong>: purchases and sales of long-term assets — negative
    here is normal for a healthy growing company (it is buying its future). <strong>Financing
    cash flow</strong>: borrowing, repaying, issuing stock, paying dividends. The layout
    reads like a diagnosis: a company whose OCF is positive, whose investing is negative
    because it builds, and whose financing swings either way is self-funding; a company with
    weak operating cash flow patched by borrowing is a patient being kept alive by
    transfusion.
  </p>
  <p>
    The key professional metric hides here: <strong>cash conversion</strong>, operating cash
    flow divided by net income. Persistently below 1 means profits that never arrive as cash
    — sales booked to customers who don't pay, costs capitalised instead of expensed, growth
    drowning in receivables. Enron, before its 2001 collapse, reported billions of net income
    while its operating cash flow quietly deteriorated; the accruals had to be manufactured,
    and they were. "Profit is an opinion, cash is a fact" is a cliché because it keeps being
    true.
  </p>

  <h2>How the statements interlock</h2>
  <p>
    The three statements are not parallel documents; they are one system. Net income from the
    income statement flows into <strong>retained earnings</strong> on the balance sheet
    (dividends leave first). Depreciation reduces both income and the book value of assets —
    but is added back in operating cash flow, because no cash left. Working-capital changes
    bridge the gap between profit and cash: when receivables rise, income statement revenue
    has already counted money the balance sheet shows the company doesn't yet have. And the
    ending cash on the cash flow statement must equal cash on the balance sheet, to the cent.
    This articulation is both the analyst's strongest tool and the fraudster's hardest
    constraint: every lie must propagate consistently through three documents, and the
    inconsistencies leak.
  </p>
  <p>
    Watch the chain work in one transaction. The company sells goods for 100, which cost it
    60, all on credit. The income statement records revenue 100 and COGS 60: profit 40. The
    balance sheet shows inventory down 60 and receivables up 100 — so assets rose 40, and
    that 40 flows into equity via retained earnings. But the cash flow statement shows
    nothing yet: no money moved. When the customer pays, receivables fall 100 and cash rises
    100; when the company restocks, cash falls and inventory rises again. One sale, three
    statements, one consistent story — and notice what the story reveals: a fast-growing
    company can report soaring profit while its cash position worsens every single quarter,
    because growth itself consumes working capital. That is not necessarily distress, but it
    is the single most common way profitable companies die.
  </p>

  <h2>Accounting methods: the choices inside the numbers</h2>
  <p>
    Nothing in a financial statement is a raw measurement; every figure is the product of
    <strong>accounting methods</strong> — conventions chosen within regulatory bounds, each
    one a place where a decade of profit can be quietly moved. The master choice is
    <strong>accrual accounting</strong> itself: revenue is recorded when <em>earned</em> and
    expenses when <em>incurred</em>, not when cash moves, so that income statements reflect
    the economic activity of the period. That principle (with its <em>matching</em>
    corollary) is what makes statements useful — and what creates every estimate below.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Revenue recognition</strong> is the most fertile ground: when a contract spans
    years (construction, software subscriptions, long service agreements), how much revenue
    belongs to this quarter? Standards (IFRS 15 / ASC 606) demand recognising revenue as
    performance obligations are satisfied, but the judgement involved let WorldCom, for
    instance, treat ordinary line costs as capital investments, inflating profit by billions
    until 2002. <strong>Depreciation</strong> spreads an asset's cost over its useful life —
    straight-line (equal slices) or accelerated (bigger early charges) — and the "useful
    life" guess directly moves reported profit. <strong>Inventory accounting</strong> offers
    a choice that matters when prices move: <em>FIFO</em> (first-in, first-out) assumes the
    oldest goods sell first, so in inflationary times it reports higher profit and higher
    inventory values; <em>LIFO</em> (last-in, first-out) reports lower, tax-sheltered profit
    — permitted under US GAAP but banned under IFRS, one visible seam between the two great
    accounting regimes. <strong>Reserves and provisions</strong> — for bad debts, warranties,
    lawsuits — are management's estimates of future pain, and are where optimism and
    "cookie-jar" smoothing both live.
  </p>
  <p>
    <em>Common misconception:</em> audited accounts are exact. They are a curated opinion —
    auditors give "reasonable assurance", not certainty, and their opinion explicitly
    concerns whether the <em>methods</em> were applied consistently, not whether the
    business will succeed. The numbers are only as honest as the estimates inside them.
  </p>

  <h2>The ratio toolkit</h2>
  <p>
    Ratios exist because raw numbers are only comparable once scaled. <strong>Profitability:</strong>
    gross margin (pricing power), operating margin (efficiency of the whole machine), and
    <strong>return on equity</strong> (net income ÷ shareholders' equity) — the summary of
    summaries, with the standing caveat that leverage inflates it: borrow to shrink the
    equity base and ROE rises even if the business worsened, which is exactly why the
    <strong>DuPont decomposition</strong> splits ROE into margin × asset turnover × leverage,
    exposing <em>which</em> engine is doing the work. <strong>Liquidity:</strong> the current
    ratio (current assets ÷ current liabilities) asks whether the company can pay the next
    twelve months' bills; below 1 invites the cash-starvation question. <strong>Leverage:</strong>
    debt-to-equity measures how aggressively the structure is financed; leverage amplifies
    good years and, because interest must be paid in every kind of year, it is the standard
    cause of death for otherwise viable firms. <strong>Efficiency:</strong> inventory days
    and receivable days measure how fast the operating cycle turns cash into goods into cash
    again — a lengthening cycle is often the first visible symptom of trouble, months before
    the income statement admits it.
  </p>

  <h2>Going deeper</h2>
  <p>
    The professional layer is <em>quality of earnings</em> analysis: reverse-engineering the
    reported numbers to score how much of profit is durable cash generation versus accrual
    artefact. Practical tells include: cash conversion persistently under 1; receivables
    growing much faster than revenue; inventory outpacing sales; serial one-off "exceptional"
    charges (which are rarely as exceptional as the adjective); and sudden changes in
    accounting policy or auditor. Watch the footnotes — leases, pension assumptions,
    off-balance-sheet entities and related-party transactions are where Enron buried its
    special-purpose vehicles, and where any modern analysis begins. Distinguish also
    <strong>cash-basis</strong> accounting (what individuals and small firms often feel like
    they use) from accrual (what companies must report), because the gap between them is
    exactly the accruals that can be managed: research by Sloan (1996) formalised this,
    showing that firms with high accruals systematically underperform later — the market
    over-extrapolates pretty accrual earnings, an anomaly that became a trading strategy.
    Finally, beware <strong>non-GAAP "adjusted" metrics</strong>: management-preferred
    measures that exclude inconvenient costs while keeping flattering revenue. The statement
    to trust is the one management cannot style: cash. The open frontier is comparability
    itself — IFRS and US GAAP have converged on much (revenue, leases) but remain apart on
    others (LIFO, development costs, goodwill impairment), so the same company reported
    under both regimes can show two different profit figures. Global analysis still begins
    by asking not just "what are the numbers?" but "whose conventions produced them?"
  </p>
  <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> Graham &amp; Dodd, Security Analysis; IFRS/GAAP frameworks; Penman, Financial Statement Analysis and Security Valuation; Sloan (1996) on accruals.</p>
